{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1583749884/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"973e499e\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nExpand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.\n- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.\n- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.\n\nRule\n- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.\n- Inside the block, write c exactly where the original input also has color c.\n- Write black everywhere else in the block.\n\nKey insight\nEach pixel selects the global mask of its own color; the result is the blockwise self-mask of the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8cc533f\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nComplete every colored sprite by applying the union of all aligned partial sprite masks.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each non-background color appears as a separated partial copy of one common sprite mask.\n- The copies share a top-left-aligned sprite window; some copies may be missing lower or right edge pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop each same-color mask to its bounding box.\n- Pad the crops to the maximum observed height and width, aligned at the top-left, and OR them into one complete template.\n- Fill that template at every sprite location using the sprite's own color.\n\nKey insight:\n- No single input sprite is complete, but their missing cells are complementary, so the aligned union reveals the full mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\" lines=\"120-136\">\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2b9ef948\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nMove the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is black.\n- One 3x3 yellow frame has a non-yellow control-color center.\n- A second control-color pixel is connected through gray path pixels to a lone yellow endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Use the vector from the second control-color pixel to the lone yellow endpoint as the frame's translation.\n- Fill the whole output with the control color.\n- Draw the translated yellow frame and extend yellow diagonal rays outward from its four corners to the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: The gray path's turns are irrelevant; its two colored endpoints encode the net displacement to apply to the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e4f6532\" lines=\"26-41\">\n<description>\nDock train sprites onto station groups by matching coupler count, then orient via divider-color markers.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg; colored stripes (edge + internal) partition grid into rectangular regions.\n- Color 8 forms train bodies; color 9 marks both isolated stations and train-attached couplers.\n- Stripe-colored pixels on trains are orientation markers.\n\nRule:\n- Stations = 8-connected 9-clusters not adjacent to any 8. Trains = components containing 8.\n- Match train to station group where |couplers| == |station size|.\n- Rotate/flip train so marker pixels face the same-color boundary stripe of the target region.\n- Translate so couplers align exactly onto station cells; erase original position.\n\nKey insight: Coupler count selects the docking target; marker color encodes which boundary stripe to face, fixing the unique orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db7260a4\" lines=\"37-56\">\n<description>\nDrop the singleton marker into the matching red U-shaped container, or make a bottom-row puddle if no closed container is hit.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background, one singleton marker pixel on the top row,\n  and one other foreground color forming vertical bars and bottom segments.\n- A closed container is a U shape: two vertical side bars with a continuous\n  bottom segment between them and an empty rectangular interior above the bottom.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top marker from its original cell.\n- If exactly one closed U-shaped container has the marker column within its\n  horizontal footprint, fill that container's empty interior with the marker color.\n- If no closed container is under the marker column, fill the whole bottom row\n  with the marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker does not identify a color by value; it identifies a vertical drop\n  column, and only a structurally closed U shape can catch and contain the fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2faf500b\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nRemove each fracture marker line and push the surrounding object pixels one cell away from it.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid with 8-connected foreground objects made from a body color and a rarer marker color.\n- Each object has a non-square bbox; the marker pixels form one complete fracture line across the shorter bbox axis.\n\nRule\n- Erase all marker pixels.\n- For a wide object, each row has one marker; body pixels left of it shift one cell left and body pixels right of it shift one cell right.\n- For a tall object, each column has one marker; body pixels above it shift one cell up and body pixels below it shift one cell down.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker line is a crack through the object, so the two sides separate by one pixel perpendicular to the crack.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f18ec8cc\" lines=\"71-86\">\n<description>\nReorder the vertical color-major blocks while preserving every block's internal pixels.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is divided into vertical blocks, each with a unique dominant color.\nSmall marker pixels from other block colors may appear inside the blocks.\n\nRule\nIf adjacent shared-color marker rows read exactly as the current block order\nfrom right to left, reverse the blocks. Otherwise move the leftmost block to\nthe far right and shift the remaining blocks left.\n\nKey insight\nThe transformation acts on whole vertical blocks, so marker pixels move with\ntheir source block instead of being recolored or independently rearranged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"981add89\" lines=\"10-29\">\n<description>\nExtend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying\n  one column and one ray color.\n- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.\n\nRule:\n- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same\n  column down to the bottom edge.\n- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.\n- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.\n- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells\nmatching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"337b420f\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nKeep the larger sprite from each separated panel and overlay those sprites into one 5x5 canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a 5x17 grid: three 5x5 panels separated by one-cell black columns.\n- Each panel has the same background color and exactly one foreground color split into two 4-connected components.\n\nRule:\n- In each panel, discard the smaller foreground component and keep the unique largest component as the sprite.\n- Process panels left to right, placing each sprite at its panel coordinates on a 5x5 background canvas.\n- If a sprite would cover an already placed sprite, slide it by the unique nearest in-bounds offset that avoids overlap.\n\nKey insight:\n- The extra same-colored component in each panel is a distractor; the final answer is the non-overlapping overlay of the three largest components.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"878187ab\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nReplace the scattered foreground markers with a bottom-left count rectangle whose two corner diagonals encode the marker counts.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid with exactly two foreground colors used as scattered marker pixels.\n- The two foreground colors have distinct pixel counts.\n\nRule\n- Ignore marker positions and use only the two foreground counts.\n- Make a 16-by-16 background canvas, fill a bottom-left red rectangle whose height is the smaller count and width is the larger count.\n- Paint yellow pixels along the two rising 45-degree diagonals from the rectangle's bottom-left and bottom-right corners.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker colors and positions vary between examples; their counts alone determine the output rectangle dimensions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"50c07299\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nMove the diagonal foreground segment to the next longer run along the same up-right diagonal.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color. The foreground pixels form a\ncontiguous one-pixel-wide segment on a single anti-diagonal.\n\nRule\nErase the current segment. If its columns are c0..c1 and its length is L, draw the same\ncolor on columns c1+1..c1+L+1 along the same anti-diagonal.\n\nKey insight\nThe segment advances past its current upper-right end by one whole segment position, and\nthe next segment is one pixel longer than the current one.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
